). They form a downward-pointing triangle-like pattern, and their colours alternate: 6 on the first new row, then 7, then 6, and so on.\n\n**The local rule.** In train pair 1 the seed row has 7s at x = 0,2,4,6,8,9,11,13,15. The first new row (y=3) has 6s at x = 1,3,5,7,10,12,14. Each of those x is an empty seed cell whose left and right neighbours (x−1 and x+1) are both coloured. So x=9 gets nothing, because x=10 is empty. Applying the same rule to row 3 gives row 5 (x = 2,4,6,11,13). Applying it again gives row 7 (3,5,12), then row 9 (4).\n\n**Why the \"own cell must be empty\" condition is needed.** Train pair 3 shows it. Its seed row has 7s at 3,5,6,7,9,10,12,13,15. Position x=6 has coloured neighbours at 5 and 7, yet it gets no 6, because x=6 is itself a 7. Only x = 4, 8, 11, 14 qualify, which matches the output exactly. Train pair 2 confirms the same thing at x=1 and x=14, which are skipped for the same reason.\n\n**When it stops.** A new row is built from the previous built row. The process ends when a row comes out empty or the next row would fall outside the grid.\n\n**On the tests.**\n- Test 1 (seed 2, 4–10, 12): the run 4–10 gives 6s only at x=3 and x=11. These two are not adjacent pairs, so the next row is empty and the process stops.\n- Test 2: a perfect alternating seed row produces a full triangle that shrinks by one cell at each end per step.\n- Test 3: the same procedure is applied to its seed row.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    rows = [y for y in range(H) if np.any(g[y] != bg)]\n    y = rows[0]\n    prev = g[y] != bg\n    colours = [6, 7]\n    k = 0\n    while y + 2 < H:\n        new = np.zeros(W, bool)\n        for x in range(1, W - 1):\n            if prev[x - 1] and prev[x + 1] and not prev[x]:\n                new[x] = True\n        if not new.any():\n            break\n        y += 2\n        out[y][new] = colours[k % 2]\n        emit(out, f\"Row {y}: colour {colours[k % 2]} placed at every gap whose two neighbours in the row above are filled.\")\n        prev = new\n        k += 1\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"They form a downward-pointing triangle-like pattern, and their colours alternate: 6 on the first new row, then 7, then 6, and so on."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Test 2: a perfect alternating seed row produces a full triangle that shrinks by one cell at each end per step."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The process ends when a row comes out empty or the next row would fall outside the grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a4c5275b9f544a18b20ec7496c6d57bc9906543055a49749d6672122d178b0d4","task_sha256":"e98170e7ec77e7c47bec586d39882883da81b3ae5efea591b3998d657f307750","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
